gineering launched in Fall 2026. This position is part of a strategic faculty expansion in the School of Engineering to hire 10 permanent faculty members, eight tenure-track and two instructional, beginning Fall 2027.   Required Qualifications   The successful candidate must hold a Ph.D. in Computer Science or a closely related discipline by the appointment start date. Applicants nearing completion may be considered if the degree is awarded before appointment. Candidates must demonstrate expertise in mobile device forensics through research, scholarship, professional experience, or a combination thereof, and the ability to teach Mobile Device Forensics, develop associated laboratory activities, mentor student research, and establish an independent research program in this area. Candidates must demonstrate strength in one or more areas of mobile device forensics: iOS or Android forensics; forensic acquisition and data recovery; mobile application analysis; mobile malware analysis; mobile memory forensics; or digital evidence analysis involving mobile devices. Mobile device forensics research, cyber forensics laboratory development, and experience pursuing external research funding are especially desirable. Experience collaborating with industry and government partners, or the ability to develop such partnerships, is also desirable.   We offer competitive salaries and a benefits package including health insurance, retirement benefits, and life insurance. Additional pay is available for summer teaching. Financial support for professional development opportunities, such as attending academic conferences, is also provided. Moving expenses are covered in whole or in part by the University.   Application Process The completed employment application must include:  A letter of interest or cover letter that addresses the candidate’s qualifications for the position. Current curriculum vitae. Unofficial transcripts documenting the conferred degree or progress toward completion 